WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:08.359
The User Library enables you to store your own custom assets, so you can use them for any project, current or upcoming.

2
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.779
Before you move on, create a new active container named Furniture.

3
00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:23.910
Let's say you are in a situation where you need an asset that is not available in the Asset Library, nor in any external library.

4
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:32.109
You can still import that asset from a DCC app of your choice, rework it, adjust its components and its materials,

5
00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:34.409
and then store it for later recall.

6
00:00:34.909 --> 00:00:38.210
You'll find the User Library at the top level of the Asset Library.

7
00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:44.869
The first time you access it, it should be mostly empty, even though it shows a few folders in this video.

8
00:00:45.759 --> 00:00:52.259
If you'd like to store a new custom asset in there, start by importing it using the Import panel in the dock.

9
00:00:54.670 --> 00:01:02.570
You have a few samples to choose from in the 112TM Basics FBX folder that's been provided to you.

10
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.519
I'll use the Chair Type A1 FBX file in this example.

11
00:01:09.109 --> 00:01:14.209
Note the information that got added to the scene graph under the Furniture container.

12
00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:19.290
You have an FBX Reference parent and a Mesh Object underneath it.

13
00:01:20.459 --> 00:01:26.359
Select the FBX parent and find it in the scene, it's probably inside the main building at this stage.

14
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.099
Move it into the Cafe area to see it better.

15
00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:34.829
It is ready to go into the User Library at this stage, but let's do one better.

16
00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:41.760
Actually, you only need the mesh, so select it and note that it's slightly sunk onto ground.

17
00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:50.879
Select the yellow center of the transformed gizmo and drag it slightly so that the chair is properly rounded on the floor of the cafe.

18
00:01:56.819 --> 00:02:00.019
Find a nice wood material that you like and apply it to the chair.

19
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.449
Now you're ready to send it to the User Library.

20
00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.669
The technique is simple.

21
00:02:10.969 --> 00:02:16.770
All you need to do is select the mesh in the scene graph, right-click and choose Add to User Library.

22
00:02:18.719 --> 00:02:22.219
If you check your User Library at this stage, you will find a new entry.

23
00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:30.810
This means that now, you don't need the original chair anymore, you can delete it from the scene, along with the FBX Reference.

24
00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:38.930
You can even delete the FBX file from the Import panel, since all you need now is to drag the new asset from the User Library into the scene.

25
00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:49.469
Let's try another one with a table, such as the file named Table Type A1 FBX.

26
00:02:53.169 --> 00:03:06.960
Use the same technique used before to relocate the table, adjust its position and its materials.

27
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.680
Select the table and send it to the User Library.

28
00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:29.159
Again, you can delete the originals and just deal with the new entity from now on.

29
00:03:30.719 --> 00:03:34.520
What's more, you can group assets to add variations.

30
00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:38.319
For example, I could duplicate the chair around the table,

31
00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:49.330
create another container below furniture named Table with two chairs,

32
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.000
and move the meshes into that container.

33
00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:02.500
Before sending the container to the User Library, you need to be aware of one fact.

34
00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:10.419
Even though the container itself is a non-renderable object, it has a pivot point or an origin point if you will.

35
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.319
By default, it is set to 0,0,0 when you create the container.

36
00:04:15.979 --> 00:04:19.379
Obviously, it is far from the table and chairs in this case.

37
00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:24.980
This would be a problem if you tried to place this piece of furniture once you add it to the User Library.

38
00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:31.430
So you can relocate the pivot point of the container using Pivot Edit on the toolbar,

39
00:04:36.279 --> 00:04:39.379
eyeball it to match the center of the table in X and Y.

40
00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.800
Once you exit Pivot Edit mode, you can send the container to the User Library.

41
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:59.740
From this moment on, you can insert a collection of tables and chairs, as well as individual parts.

42
00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:08.259
Keep in mind that these assets are saved to disk as physical files, this means you can copy them from one system to another.

43
00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:16.509
By default, user libraries are saved to your Documents folder in the C drive, but that can be changed to InMotion preferences.

44
00:05:19.709 --> 00:05:27.610
At any rate, custom assets are saved as .tmi files and can be easily copied from one system to the next.

45
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:35.800
If you'd like to try this out, you'll find the User Library folder provided to you in the zip archive you downloaded for this course.

46
00:05:36.779 --> 00:05:43.779
Copy the two subfolders named Furniture and QT onto your own User Library folder on your C drive.

47
00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:53.220
You will need to save your project and restart to InMotion, but once you do, you'll have a new set of furniture you can use to populate the cafe.

48
00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.040
In the next video, we'll look at Path Animation.
